WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.283 --> 00:00:03.821
A LangGraph agent runs on your laptop in an afternoon.

2
00:00:03.821 --> 00:00:08.603
Getting it in front of a whole organization, safely, is the real project.

3
00:00:08.912 --> 00:00:14.462
In this video, we follow one set of agents from a developer's laptop into Microsoft Foundry.

4
00:00:14.462 --> 00:00:24.294
Along the way, we answer the six questions an enterprise architect will ask, and I'll show you where each answer lives in the Foundry portal, and how to get there.

5
00:00:26.202 --> 00:00:29.204
LangGraph is where the agent's behaviour lives.

6
00:00:29.204 --> 00:00:32.844
Each agent is a graph of small steps sharing typed state.

7
00:00:32.844 --> 00:00:36.101
Some steps are plain Python, and some call a model.

8
00:00:37.073 --> 00:00:40.969
One step pauses the whole graph for a person to approve a change.

9
00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:44.924
The state is saved, and the run picks up exactly where it stopped.

10
00:00:45.906 --> 00:00:51.730
Because the model can be scripted out, close to 700 tests run in seconds, with no network.

11
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.420
LangGraph isn't the only choice.

12
00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:03.030
Microsoft Agent Framework is one alternative, the successor to Semantic Kernel and AutoGen, with graph-based workflows of its own.

13
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:07.003
I chose LangGraph for its explicit graphs and checkpointing.

14
00:01:07.003 --> 00:01:10.050
The rest of this story is the same either way.

15
00:01:10.763 --> 00:01:13.383
On a laptop, none of these has an answer.

16
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:14.533
Where does it run?

17
00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:15.172
Who is it?

18
00:01:15.172 --> 00:01:16.194
Who can call it?

19
00:01:16.194 --> 00:01:17.663
Where do people use it?

20
00:01:17.663 --> 00:01:18.366
Is it safe?

21
00:01:18.366 --> 00:01:20.091
And can we see what it did?

22
00:01:20.091 --> 00:01:22.710
An enterprise architect will ask all six.

23
00:01:23.327 --> 00:01:30.963
Microsoft Foundry is Microsoft's platform for building and running AI apps and agents, and there's more than one way in.

24
00:01:30.963 --> 00:01:38.026
The simplest is a model deployment: your own app calls the model, and all of the agent logic stays in your app.

25
00:01:38.276 --> 00:01:44.527
A prompt agent is configured in the portal: instructions, a model and tools, with no code at all.

26
00:01:44.781 --> 00:01:51.565
A workflow agent chains prompt agents together, with branches and approval steps, built visually or in YAML.

27
00:01:51.823 --> 00:01:58.783
And a hosted agent is your own code, in a container that Foundry runs for you, whatever framework is inside.

28
00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:02.262
A hosted agent does its orchestration in its own code.

29
00:02:02.262 --> 00:02:04.389
Here, that's the LangGraph graph.

30
00:02:04.644 --> 00:02:09.068
Guardrails apply to every model call, whichever way in you choose.

31
00:02:09.068 --> 00:02:13.157
Agents also get tracing, monitoring and evaluations built in.

32
00:02:13.157 --> 00:02:19.457
With a plain model deployment, your app opts in, through OpenTelemetry and the evaluation SDK.

33
00:02:19.457 --> 00:02:23.076
These agents are code first, so they're hosted agents.

34
00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:32.619
A build step turns each agent into a deployable folder: the graph, a small entry point, and its requirements.

35
00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:38.573
Then one azd deploy publishes all 23, and Foundry builds each container in the cloud.

36
00:02:38.845 --> 00:02:41.733
Here's the project in the Foundry portal.

37
00:02:41.733 --> 00:02:44.832
The left navigation follows an agent's life.

38
00:02:44.832 --> 00:02:47.861
Create holds agents, models and guardrails.

39
00:02:47.861 --> 00:02:50.255
Observe has monitoring and traces.

40
00:02:50.255 --> 00:02:52.861
And Improve has data and evaluations.

41
00:02:53.115 --> 00:02:55.005
Under Create, select Agents.

42
00:02:55.005 --> 00:02:56.219
All 23 are listed.

43
00:02:56.219 --> 00:03:01.819
Each one is a hosted agent, it is running, and every deployment gets a new version.

44
00:03:03.764 --> 00:03:05.465
Select an agent to open it.

45
00:03:05.465 --> 00:03:09.432
It opens in the playground, where you can try it straight away.

46
00:03:09.432 --> 00:03:11.572
The Details tab shows how it runs.

47
00:03:14.125 --> 00:03:18.813
Under Identity & access, the agent has its own Entra agent identity.

48
00:03:18.813 --> 00:03:25.706
That identity is granted access to the project's models, so no keys or secrets ever sit in the code.

49
00:03:25.706 --> 00:03:31.427
That answers questions one and two: it runs in Foundry, and it is its own identity.

50
00:03:32.391 --> 00:03:36.923
Under Endpoints, every agent exposes a standard Responses endpoint.

51
00:03:36.923 --> 00:03:41.996
Only people with the right Azure role can call it, using their own sign-in.

52
00:03:41.996 --> 00:03:47.069
So who can call it is decided by Azure role-based access, not by the agent.

53
00:03:48.035 --> 00:03:50.493
So how do people actually reach it?

54
00:03:50.493 --> 00:03:56.109
Under Channels, Foundry can publish an agent to Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot.

55
00:03:56.109 --> 00:04:01.795
That needs tenant permissions I don't have here, so I built the front end myself.

56
00:04:05.783 --> 00:04:07.586
Lumen is a simple chat app.

57
00:04:07.586 --> 00:04:13.863
It calls the agent's endpoint with the user's own Azure sign-in, and sends any attached files.

58
00:04:13.863 --> 00:04:19.671
Here, a business analyst hands the requirements agent a business requirements document.

59
00:04:26.902 --> 00:04:31.766
Back come structured requirements, with ambiguities and gaps flagged for review.

60
00:04:31.766 --> 00:04:37.542
It also caught an instruction hidden in the document, and a section the content filter blocked.

61
00:04:37.542 --> 00:04:39.062
More on that in a moment.

62
00:04:40.699 --> 00:04:42.730
Some agents pause for a person.

63
00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:49.083
The interface documentation agent drafts an interface control document from the specs it's given.

64
00:05:02.288 --> 00:05:05.659
When it needs a decision, Lumen shows an approval card.

65
00:05:05.659 --> 00:05:10.011
The architect approves, and the agent resumes exactly where it stopped.

66
00:05:14.148 --> 00:05:16.794
On the code side, it's one provider file.

67
00:05:16.794 --> 00:05:22.601
It builds the request, calls the Responses endpoint, and sends the approval decision back.

68
00:05:22.601 --> 00:05:24.666
Any front end could do the same.

69
00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:25.634
Is it safe?

70
00:05:25.634 --> 00:05:28.866
Back in the portal, Guardrails is also under Create.

71
00:05:28.866 --> 00:05:35.702
A guardrail is attached to the model deployment, so it checks every call to that model, from any agent or app.

72
00:05:35.955 --> 00:05:38.881
Open the default guardrail to see its controls.

73
00:05:38.881 --> 00:05:44.979
It blocks harmful content, detects jailbreaks, and checks documents for indirect prompt injection.

74
00:05:45.235 --> 00:05:46.930
Guardrails are one layer.

75
00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:48.353
The other is testing.

76
00:05:48.353 --> 00:05:51.945
The AI Red Teaming Agent attacks each deployed agent.

77
00:05:51.945 --> 00:06:02.110
At the time of recording, its reports open in the classic Foundry view, so switch New Foundry off, then select Evaluation, and the AI red teaming tab.

78
00:06:14.558 --> 00:06:18.710
Each scan hides attacks inside the documents the agent reads.

79
00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.956
Open the requirements agent scan.

80
00:06:20.956 --> 00:06:24.564
It found one real failure, in the agent you just saw.

81
00:06:28.019 --> 00:06:35.593
The fix is layered: the content filter, a prompt rule, and a deterministic scan that drops anything built from harmful text.

82
00:06:35.869 --> 00:06:41.587
Back in the list, the re-scan after the fix found nothing, and the case is now part of the test suite.

83
00:06:43.891 --> 00:06:46.401
Last question: can we see what it did?

84
00:06:46.401 --> 00:06:52.081
Switch back to the new Foundry, open the agent from Agents, and select its Traces tab.

85
00:06:52.081 --> 00:06:54.921
Every request is traced with OpenTelemetry.

86
00:07:17.298 --> 00:07:23.805
Open a trace, and you see each LangGraph step, and every model call underneath, with its timing and tokens.

87
00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:36.314
Under Observe, Monitor rolls it up across all the agents: runs, tokens, and what it actually costs.

88
00:07:36.314 --> 00:07:40.318
You get all of this, whatever framework is inside the container.

89
00:07:54.951 --> 00:07:56.467
So, the six questions.

90
00:07:56.467 --> 00:07:58.258
It runs as a hosted agent.

91
00:07:58.258 --> 00:08:00.257
It is its own Entra identity.

92
00:08:00.257 --> 00:08:02.668
Azure roles decide who can call it.

93
00:08:02.668 --> 00:08:06.251
People reach it through Lumen, or Teams and Copilot.

94
00:08:06.251 --> 00:08:09.007
Guardrails and red teaming keep it safe.

95
00:08:09.007 --> 00:08:12.039
And traces and the monitor show what it did.

96
00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:14.502
So where does Foundry fit?

97
00:08:14.502 --> 00:08:17.520
Your framework decides how the agent behaves.

98
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:22.750
Foundry decides how it runs: hosting, identity, access, safety and visibility.

99
00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.366
And people reach it however suits them.

100
00:08:26.178 --> 00:08:28.013
From laptop to enterprise.

101
00:08:28.013 --> 00:08:30.765
LangGraph agents, on Microsoft Foundry.
